id: rust.app-state
kind: rust
title: AppState (Arc<AppState>)
aliases: app state, shared state, state struct
tags: architecture, async
questions:
  - how do you share a database pool and config between handlers?
invariant: One state value is built at startup, wrapped in Arc and cloned cheaply into every handler and task.
answer_30s: Build a single AppState at startup (pools, clients, config, metrics), share it as Arc<AppState>, and keep mutability inside the components rather than in the struct.
answer_2m: |
  Why one struct: dependencies become explicit in signatures, construction
  happens once with real validation (fail fast at boot, not on first request), and
  there are no globals. The interior-mutability rule keeps concurrency sane: a
  pool, HTTP client and metrics registry are internally synchronised, so
  AppState itself is immutable and needs no lock. If AppState starts growing
  Arc<Mutex<T>>, that is a signal the state wants a single owner behind a
  channel. For tests, AppState is the seam: build it with fakes and handlers are
  testable without a server.
when_to_use:
  - every server: the dependency container, built once at startup
avoid_when:
  - mutable global caches with their own lifecycle (own them in a task with a channel)
minimal_example: |
  #[derive(Clone)]
  struct AppState {
      db: DbPool,            // internally synchronised
      http: reqwest::Client, // pool and timeouts configured once
      cfg: Arc<Config>,      // immutable snapshot
      metrics: Arc<Metrics>,
  }

  let state = AppState { db, http, cfg: Arc::new(cfg), metrics };
  let _ = app(state.clone());
related:
  - rust.axum
  - rust.arc
  - design.shared-state
  - rust.connection-pool
sources:
  - https://docs.rs/axum/latest/axum/extract/struct.State.html
